#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100638 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3433cbc0-fe8c-5b67-a888-4df13b4b68c3" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Smooth Function"}] :question/id #uuid "c6ce8503-265f-5a02-93f4-ad99390c2734" :question/math-academy-id "e-333" :question/problem "The graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $f'(x).$\n\n![](images/d9/d9746bb18a0e3ae662e477173e4da8b6599245edb02003287d83ab5e894aa814.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) <             0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x \\approx 2.$ So $f''(2)\\approx0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y=f'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $f'(2)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x \\approx 2,$ the slope is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8f8c622296689efa9290c0ecb219abdba20cf71052ca77df1a131658d534a68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100639 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2ef7a61-79b9-5464-ac2a-3afb7dddd1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "68f7ad36-cab7-5ffe-99d9-e720bbf7971a" :question/math-academy-id "e-334" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown below. Sketch the graph of $y=f''(x).$\n\n![](images/bf/bf8bde9f9b5060fbf14a329833f35ce9970c850e805e0c551cccaa70d6288c06.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x) = f'(x)$ then $f''(x)=g'(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y=g'(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the critical points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $g'(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $g'(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $g'(x) < 0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the critical points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=g(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{not extr.}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $g'$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x \\approx 1$ and $x\\approx 2.25.$ At the inflection points, we have $g''(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y=g'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x\\approx 1$ and $x\\approx 2.25.$\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $g'(1)$ and $g'(2.25),$ but from the graph of $y=g(x),$ we can conclude that at $x \\approx 1$ and $x\\approx 2.25$ the slope is zero and negative, respectively.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=g'(x)=f''(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/78/785b221ae245b389e707f8189c91e57af888ecbb44dfce384ab1c60958d91a6f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100762 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa025d7b-74d5-5627-a7cd-12e3681b8b82" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Quadratic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "98a83d94-0f65-5475-850e-c1abd8836e15" :question/math-academy-id "e-6819" :question/problem "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below. Sketch the graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x).$\n\n![](images/1f/1fcbb5677ad7c8f630ccd7d194ef92f59fe3424d6bdb85e20f1ae522567b94ec.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) <             0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty,\\infty).$ So, $f''(x) >  0$ and $y=f'(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/15/1567dcdd3dac0eb6b76b71e20466a77457a3d956c85be96048e5d3a94568c03a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100763 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97710476-c327-5b2d-9675-10f36b967d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Piecewise-Differentiable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0b633f45-0fdb-5d4b-90e0-51c6628ce360" :question/math-academy-id "e-6820" :question/problem "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below. Sketch the graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x).$\n\n![](images/ed/ed26d7560d9bd7294bf35a8e0e52292a7b5922d419303c63430c73c32c63070e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following: \n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get $f'(x) <         0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $\\textrm{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also:\n\n- For $x \\in (-\\infty,-1),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is an increasing straight line. So, $y=f'(x)$ will be a positive constant on this interval.\n\n- For $x \\in (-1,\\infty),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down. So, $f''(x) <  0$ and $y=f'(x)$ is decreasing on this interval.\n\nAdditionally, we have a rapid change of the slope of the tangent line when we pass through $x=-1$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=-1,$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y=f'(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d00aadf7b1666ea8a762833d1844d638a224759c58d1b06929657ddb90dc4abb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa025d7b-74d5-5627-a7cd-12e3681b8b82" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62979a52-85d5-5774-9455-23278176b662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8eeeac56e5dcab52f594a4c882d92710b7007f4cdeff4e64f24d48a45dc5bb08.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505303} {:answer/id #uuid "925143de-4bf2-5ff9-adeb-a9bfef450030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8b9a32fc56bf649ca75be0ce526585b5943401ed6c452433660da97b538a0b9b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505387} {:answer/id #uuid "968dc5de-a83e-5002-a0b6-ae97c0d66b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/5772003758d93364017c58703c7524f5f609589b15fa99fd1173b75a45ccca29.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505394} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a96176-6931-5b3d-afbb-496f2b08f7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5d/5d356f03f2f197ec47e77559a1680c2e12952cd057366622ea6ec9ff168e2fe1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505453} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5c63f4-cb4f-5bb1-af10-a9894d6a6c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0c6a4555975ea8098911704ac04b304b3f235cd64ad15012d0eb55bdf9fc810e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "925143de-4bf2-5ff9-adeb-a9bfef450030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8b9a32fc56bf649ca75be0ce526585b5943401ed6c452433660da97b538a0b9b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505387} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cfef9e0-fbf8-52ea-bf53-d515b624d700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62b94dd7-cfc2-59b5-b47b-429c83f35bf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114573" :question/problem "![](images/ca/caffdfa7bc25bf9c5fc21b8f385c8e8dfd3ad6524ae51729135fac16b9a38792.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Of the following, which could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following: \n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8b9a32fc56bf649ca75be0ce526585b5943401ed6c452433660da97b538a0b9b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa025d7b-74d5-5627-a7cd-12e3681b8b82" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424d0541-3986-5328-a50f-2382f1d98417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d3af805dcfbfad183dd50c9ce7654cee9142e61ac41f79fe44d1212a636f9d94.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505235} {:answer/id #uuid "62714582-555d-5e26-a461-854ac6245d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/96c9048f06178220bd443594ebe90b570cea1017faf9eb5207111f74604fa4be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505302} {:answer/id #uuid "78e1e403-872f-5281-a201-3491e337f8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/74e843a3f59d4b0af115b948f0d929be106142fdf146c1befc226419e98f9372.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505350} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1c47a9-5056-518a-95ee-d284e33e8200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/22f1c9da3a225f641aadfc6e244e54fbf49a22e58724b937c73502181f91681c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505471} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e5e598-abff-5bb2-aeb0-e10923fb9cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/35/353e94e8a1c94ece1b9f8b432e9115eebe2520848717aa4049382c121d234a5a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd1c47a9-5056-518a-95ee-d284e33e8200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/22f1c9da3a225f641aadfc6e244e54fbf49a22e58724b937c73502181f91681c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505471} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ad2a19-9ee2-55dd-b140-b09c354ee276" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "afcdf6e0-b120-55bc-9035-712918708d21" :question/math-academy-id "q-114593" :question/problem "![](images/34/347ddfa6a11cd4904f0e790a55a611edbb6ac89d4a4a2190627bd318ad2af7dd.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0.5)$ | $0.5$ | $(0.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/22/22f1c9da3a225f641aadfc6e244e54fbf49a22e58724b937c73502181f91681c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3433cbc0-fe8c-5b67-a888-4df13b4b68c3" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Smooth Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065c16e1-4085-50a6-bb78-007ef4782272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/50/5045b953f12139167ac6498f8d379b0b89379344a1e3f04f555d35c39e87248a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505116} {:answer/id #uuid "b6478e87-3a1c-58dd-9745-b0944fd5ebba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0cd84966048643abfd83c950d49ec7d9318c177cfdf062f58c502f7d966c7c29.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505459} {:answer/id #uuid "e12dd70e-42cb-57e7-94b7-a9810647facf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a1/a107d619837beb87b1690ac8cc6ea500f374de3d5d4138d5a6a29ff32daf6ac9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505544} {:answer/id #uuid "e71f34a5-a7ea-5111-80bb-b97aaedf2b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/57e2244806cb3b1168da1a1be30f923b7ee721431addd926654fcb4e51c2063b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505557} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7487c3-4a91-5600-8a62-a7fa78decdbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d41ffdc4ea9c6bb9b3dfb66924aabe95a497f26e0cca6b97a3a533ea8a864bd3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e71f34a5-a7ea-5111-80bb-b97aaedf2b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/57e2244806cb3b1168da1a1be30f923b7ee721431addd926654fcb4e51c2063b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505557} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cd5fc7e-847e-5ca9-aca9-1c3905f8545b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1749906-d3a8-5b9b-9937-44d64a8b3739" :question/math-academy-id "q-114615" :question/problem "![](images/09/09c3ef18cefb953df4469efa496bc8bdd57b0923c1a19842a8384b6d54ee78f1.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈1.5.$ So ${f}^{″}(1.5)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(1.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈1.5,$ the slope is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57e2244806cb3b1168da1a1be30f923b7ee721431addd926654fcb4e51c2063b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3433cbc0-fe8c-5b67-a888-4df13b4b68c3" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Smooth Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58a4ac8a-9ad5-59f6-8074-8fc6edc9b565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/26aa00905a5decb29f8f5a71825b225390392333d4be375e8f5018fea7c44fc9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505283} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea84576-2e0d-5ee2-8f21-06257827a47b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e73b329105ffe0c8fa1b4f4e9c18a2015d135b37019074de350dd7a57c10d5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505357} {:answer/id #uuid "94287a7b-ef3c-5924-8031-1edcb4ad269b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cc/cc425d19b4f25ddea6e52dd5267ce895aa1598dc19085eef0f9d832188f77f71.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505388} {:answer/id #uuid "db0be5f6-e260-5fed-988b-f8affb29f9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bd/bd4a3c1360880a5c39f2f0366bbfeae264998515845c7dfb097d9803aa4a3ccc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505525} {:answer/id #uuid "e5cc33d7-500a-5599-9848-8394db27e68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6b/6b738f1d76c14e03e048ca751dbfa416f05e415ca3cf1729fef6821d7d44d00f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5cc33d7-500a-5599-9848-8394db27e68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6b/6b738f1d76c14e03e048ca751dbfa416f05e415ca3cf1729fef6821d7d44d00f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505556} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c5472a0-c79b-54dc-863f-15b3152ef90d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c2bbcbb-181d-5929-9b37-8caf2befb3ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-114617" :question/problem "![](images/82/82edcc0447d4a2f6237c810c474e72369d8a7f1df7b75b7d1cee575dc6648f87.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Of the following, which could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/6b/6b738f1d76c14e03e048ca751dbfa416f05e415ca3cf1729fef6821d7d44d00f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97710476-c327-5b2d-9675-10f36b967d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Piecewise-Differentiable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3defae3c-884f-5ea8-80de-1e621559316b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ffb86bfbcedbb021c61571aefce757752ff5d395629a8717e348bd173cd06b30.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505219} {:answer/id #uuid "71818e85-b7aa-5b18-873a-26dbd767a46b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4aa24ed382e7591e79ae293316c2ce1b976a3ea4cf87a602e7577e5de2d44e14.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505336} {:answer/id #uuid "90fddf5f-5fed-55db-ae50-af4e3de480a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/88f858ca236aabbf86103dbd6a5227821e7b2bf9611dde0872a6a1e34bc6b931.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505382} {:answer/id #uuid "a559e948-fc3e-5a00-97da-c0675be541c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/64/64e6bc97e55e414cfa9bfe2003cc6bcf0be546e657bf8397535974d4b1861d74.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505437} {:answer/id #uuid "ca22e61b-de36-50ae-8128-7bf2c8de7e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/68/68e94fb9500dc51ed5dbae1d1987ff8dc2450d3cc8ad91e3161d3d367631f173.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90fddf5f-5fed-55db-ae50-af4e3de480a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/88f858ca236aabbf86103dbd6a5227821e7b2bf9611dde0872a6a1e34bc6b931.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505382} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd00428-35f0-519d-8d68-450f9941cf18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b0e787a-88a1-5ceb-86de-c3a98e9d2857" :question/math-academy-id "q-114643" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d69caa35281c084636fbbf530b40c264ad4cdd7e626f941be67d3c0e0405a4ba.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is constant, we get ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $\\text{const.}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $0$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from positive to  $0$) when we pass through $x=3$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=3$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/88f858ca236aabbf86103dbd6a5227821e7b2bf9611dde0872a6a1e34bc6b931.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97710476-c327-5b2d-9675-10f36b967d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Piecewise-Differentiable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b33ec3-5950-5c5d-bc79-771c3f1f6633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f6/f68914b468166f15e7529357c336fec2d47337b6dac58ae105ee7f368d2b78d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505141} {:answer/id #uuid "31557e3b-d4b0-5267-aa9a-7e150496c677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/466a429d3a5bac24bf0f8bc8a67750fec14d7b7ce211fc0c6bc429881fa7c923.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505191} {:answer/id #uuid "3e94616f-6a31-55bf-b92a-1fa270b2ee0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df71cb03d76b04cb315575fc91fe0c0f55635abe790684773e56d780ab66a0a4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505223} {:answer/id #uuid "a044db3e-be10-5ff0-bb44-4b2a8ffcbe05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b40a9c3fb1a90f5e25a7bd2d95fca4e36c378317f1b41c48ae7cd53a1d2c5e3d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505420} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb2bdd5-9d4f-5154-85fe-9b53c449bdda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7bef73fc655d0953a315bdd6f9524d0527e54c65453f3359059df7d43c444de7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbb2bdd5-9d4f-5154-85fe-9b53c449bdda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7bef73fc655d0953a315bdd6f9524d0527e54c65453f3359059df7d43c444de7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505597} :answer-field/id #uuid "d848507f-dcf4-5e8d-8a83-e80e1621fe7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63a13aa0-4488-51d5-bbbc-b4c2f1626c48" :question/math-academy-id "q-114646" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1daad2a8baabf38dabf1dfe5f8d7e0edf4f3ac8645145f2d0513fc2c59fc26e0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is constant, we get ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\text{const.}$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $0$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from  $0$ to negative)  when we pass through $x=0$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=0$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/7b/7bef73fc655d0953a315bdd6f9524d0527e54c65453f3359059df7d43c444de7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97710476-c327-5b2d-9675-10f36b967d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Piecewise-Differentiable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109603d2-dcfe-5abe-b525-db503e1c609e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/17ccf1c0863b25fd8cb0d4227c43c071e3e24f35831ae1eed6c262aca353ba08.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505132} {:answer/id #uuid "6580ea04-9e4d-5252-a7be-8ca4d068a89b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/9017bf057696001853904341f5479243a29ffe4faa435039499f32e7f90ad594.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505310} {:answer/id #uuid "a533fdf3-5be1-5597-b740-16199c88b5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/048a95c8960c30ef089b3d69032a5423e7ae11663bd1eb7ff97815298b667e9a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505435} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c32281-4d07-5460-a1b2-3495c04c942a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/99/9973f597f4d21f5e17b58939279aa1b4c5e25d890bcea5aad32129202d571f26.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505441} {:answer/id #uuid "ef050412-4c8f-52e9-b6a2-3a607cf09959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/01f0a91835e9337feac6340286c599b5ef29ac307bc29acc9a4a159c3d58d4f5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "109603d2-dcfe-5abe-b525-db503e1c609e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/17ccf1c0863b25fd8cb0d4227c43c071e3e24f35831ae1eed6c262aca353ba08.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505132} :answer-field/id #uuid "62a3a174-4905-5d32-9ded-ef2a2049d3eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15879841-b72a-52ec-bd4a-969c8835b3a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-114648" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1de00f79b0621d8b9f5f5731f4796bd53d3319451deca953bc12cfb4c24f65d.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from negative to positive) when we pass through $x=3$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=3$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/17/17ccf1c0863b25fd8cb0d4227c43c071e3e24f35831ae1eed6c262aca353ba08.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2ef7a61-79b9-5464-ac2a-3afb7dddd1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e44565-9c53-5bb8-9cc9-7639994a6273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2d/2dc2c5bc8ca4e173a3ee4b853aa85666b2761cebedfdb8ae7038b794ce86af1c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505105} {:answer/id #uuid "4b47436b-6b10-5b3c-bd03-8dfc22297805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d1ff02104c55267fc08b7721707af650ded99120109acad65e95c00861c90e69.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505256} {:answer/id #uuid "7f071171-1572-5433-8c60-46d61cf139aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4f/4f0e941cff224d51f4b92ce4913c53f0b9e4346e7a8a5382d0259637da6321a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505359} {:answer/id #uuid "ba57dcc5-0a7f-5a7a-8029-90e9d6d4094e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b6f488650c668f015b88651a7ae9604b1d56bca6c268b90e4c5db96d406cf4d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505463} {:answer/id #uuid "fb74d49e-0382-5626-bafa-cc9547fa60bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2d/2d8ab7a5804de834dd6640e1cb7ba9e8ec13c6e1d36ef5bb6b0115d8eaa00095.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb74d49e-0382-5626-bafa-cc9547fa60bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2d/2d8ab7a5804de834dd6640e1cb7ba9e8ec13c6e1d36ef5bb6b0115d8eaa00095.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505596} :answer-field/id #uuid "760b0e18-9ea3-5b0b-8bc6-06ccc419b799" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fa7b9a4-0348-582a-97ef-22ec70d27b82" :question/math-academy-id "q-114743" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2a62b8616e1178cc7200c91e4e45366b5aece9d7e9838f02b9c60217fd79f8b.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=g(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=g(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ and $y={g}^{′}(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d8ab7a5804de834dd6640e1cb7ba9e8ec13c6e1d36ef5bb6b0115d8eaa00095.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2ef7a61-79b9-5464-ac2a-3afb7dddd1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e8d76b-3ba8-547f-a653-d5044bad5019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/55/5522d27e0e17ab88e39c9559581f0ba272ee8ceef9c969466bb73505e9c3bf26.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505174} {:answer/id #uuid "693905f1-da25-590a-b87e-28d3be5fa75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e5214bcdbcd4d596234d2e9900d35fc1a43b4363f8162abe3df6a42d1f1b7bde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505319} {:answer/id #uuid "a2616917-5ff5-518e-8534-b133d986511c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f6/f6c4aca7259e3fe0999202dfcf0e572238b9bfd8263699768c65df89daafc8aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505428} {:answer/id #uuid "a5180969-fdc1-5e5f-a0f6-9864b26cdc4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/73/73f02c15c2bb60918b4dfdea5f7f33a86101393b9f58e64df800b2887c67aa2e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505433} {:answer/id #uuid "e528abc0-6090-578f-a779-379c3d3c17ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c4ffc73403729de6b9869bb70f1b466a3d426a615615e0f80d15293e97320c6b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e528abc0-6090-578f-a779-379c3d3c17ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c4ffc73403729de6b9869bb70f1b466a3d426a615615e0f80d15293e97320c6b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505552} :answer-field/id #uuid "b46c5d02-2ff2-520b-818a-fba2dd2a7ca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e2c85a10-a5e4-538c-9cb8-85994d6879ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-114754" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdf32d536487a7927752daa6ea293244ca217f528881630827bc85d6db5044d2.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the critical points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the critical points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has an inflection point at $x≈2.$ At the inflection point, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈2.$\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${g}^{′}(2)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangent to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈2$ is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below. \n\n![](images/c4/c4ffc73403729de6b9869bb70f1b466a3d426a615615e0f80d15293e97320c6b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2ef7a61-79b9-5464-ac2a-3afb7dddd1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "131edabd-fe45-5f12-a92e-17e4a00daca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/856eb2e47ccb4aada8bb3be5c44f2c1b6bbee298b607946d7694cc259e4f7786.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505138} {:answer/id #uuid "64e53d3a-08ff-5a45-8e4f-5b7915cc9e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e9/e9aeba3b197425ddf7f98e36096be174f54f76c6c1ac5aea453c63de72e01a3d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505308} {:answer/id #uuid "6997daeb-c0f2-57ac-a20c-08939ec9a4ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/7299c08ee83650af2291aecbacdf40a3c142141bfbb1109afa07cb4269a00fce.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505320} {:answer/id #uuid "6b31e19d-bc9f-5077-a168-b009d12bcc44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/99/9902d1174794b89cc6ed4f4c002cead71e4625a4bc3bcb1d04881e1903882fde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505322} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0a17be-f006-5693-bab4-bcdbb6905ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/4289eb05ab23d48f2727848724d3cd2f130cdb5fe9d92d569f3abd05b534c0e2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc0a17be-f006-5693-bab4-bcdbb6905ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/4289eb05ab23d48f2727848724d3cd2f130cdb5fe9d92d569f3abd05b534c0e2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505530} :answer-field/id #uuid "57227157-500d-5351-8239-220e276379f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "60da6948-77d4-526b-84a9-f5ff89c1d7b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114769" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c51543f8cf91203bd242fd62fec82936788b54490466c2af25c995f7a25af8cf.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has inflection points at $x≈0.5$ and $x≈1.5.$ At the inflection points, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈0$ and $x≈2.$ \n\nWe don't know the exact values of ${g}^{′}(0.5)$ and ${g}^{′}(1.5)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangents to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈0.5$ and $x≈1.5$ are  negative and positive, respectively.  \n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/42/4289eb05ab23d48f2727848724d3cd2f130cdb5fe9d92d569f3abd05b534c0e2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3433cbc0-fe8c-5b67-a888-4df13b4b68c3" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Smooth Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "127803c3-67f4-5702-8fea-c46c384133c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6f76708a1a70a14e10ce6b041186f4610f4a4b4c5b32ba4ac4dc3d310f6fdd17.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505135} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4a53ea-9228-5174-a635-63c1d66e653b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5e/5ec0265dbebeeff2efb1ccb8d8e1d2d74a213fad091872b4366445a66a36bacc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505181} {:answer/id #uuid "44244673-86b2-5305-94b0-6980c7258aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b584eceb1fc1189e1a17b936222a3acb48987d319dab42a70c24aebb99df3a64.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505241} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c327c0-d3e9-5c13-b2a4-68c7efadd0fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d0/d0c1765a4b35cbbfc8fdb4ee6182937d7abcd53aa779d63066f6b7937128bec3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505518} {:answer/id #uuid "dc080b55-562f-5acc-913f-1c61a64683d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3f43012be791a13962a3b41cab736c04f2ad9fe7ad965af5e8ea23bf1dfa07cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5c327c0-d3e9-5c13-b2a4-68c7efadd0fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d0/d0c1765a4b35cbbfc8fdb4ee6182937d7abcd53aa779d63066f6b7937128bec3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505518} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf15827c-2aaf-5f53-bd58-4814c2cd9492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "beaea1c2-cac1-535c-a0ba-94e307b8fec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-16216" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1ae30d99b72eaa6267e28951f87b77d0ed02b1a64d59efce03a9708090b00ca3.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0c1765a4b35cbbfc8fdb4ee6182937d7abcd53aa779d63066f6b7937128bec3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3433cbc0-fe8c-5b67-a888-4df13b4b68c3" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Smooth Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2effdf9-1172-58d4-b628-4c933cc6ca44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2f/2f85d0d358a179eb7be4e13bd9d8a8d787e48c24971fd3f65d1efb38859c5a32.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099128} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5bf606-62bc-5e83-a623-1d665ec7536d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7a/7adc74d7e58a8b8a259db58189a09ccf02deafaa8882d7bfd093ad318da9f40a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099129} {:answer/id #uuid "058cd11c-3503-5168-a848-aa5e9e4b6340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/141efeb601a2cd56f51a3e2262d96548ab77261e89d70150b60e3805b6ca07c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099130} {:answer/id #uuid "20d830d8-9525-5ed2-90a1-62c8c574e903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9e12ffc6f7e4e4b7c81a39f79999255e5851ba9bee9eea30f30e3e17f8b10216.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099131} {:answer/id #uuid "52e4731d-e6bc-51ec-9c8b-05af499902ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bcba5dedbe0de806c72d8b989ab963f03992d2aac78958302c0b8263628988e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52e4731d-e6bc-51ec-9c8b-05af499902ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bcba5dedbe0de806c72d8b989ab963f03992d2aac78958302c0b8263628988e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099132} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdc655ac-b184-5aa7-84eb-5202af0e6ddf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12ca6b93-de58-502b-8ecb-6b5fc6d679b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-20235" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a44ff10b8b99077e742024dc360563e2481871a44592f1fd9260947dba82dfed.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈1.5.$ So ${f}^{″}(1.5)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(1.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈1.5,$ the slope is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/af/affedc1cf175ac4fb632345fcf45d5facbc29ecbe6ce5f4a2c31800a22e2e170.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3433cbc0-fe8c-5b67-a888-4df13b4b68c3" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Smooth Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8430c48e-2188-5143-878f-214905f90d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3b/3b86a0a6f67463532a765c3ce85cb58da229ffa4e83ffd755610371e6c089b2b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505366} {:answer/id #uuid "e40399cc-bbdb-5f72-924c-c9b32bbede3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/797689e3182197fe54b91672806c38f2114c947b1454d4c7545d20c6f91e73bd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505549} {:answer/id #uuid "efcae7e2-c062-5950-b721-7fc049630ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7c/7c357d99f2475caf07dc009d58743d4cc266811cfbecfc79d3d05cb1fbbe37f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505572} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a39322-d78c-5b92-9813-d64c89655e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/093a103de253567e4479c973c013d99d9e9788a746517e3adc480ce55b146a72.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505586} {:answer/id #uuid "f978c937-d8e6-58e3-8836-cd731d7bc6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/96a710a53000c6bd973d193343fe564f9f9487e5348a936d44fce334a8dbb6fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8430c48e-2188-5143-878f-214905f90d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3b/3b86a0a6f67463532a765c3ce85cb58da229ffa4e83ffd755610371e6c089b2b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505366} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f4f5425-2516-5054-8553-6855f1c18d2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bee43d68-1db4-514c-9b20-dd6368dba765" :question/math-academy-id "q-20238" :question/problem "![](images/63/6364cdaa6ace7feb4b8ed53dc30738f019e97a915e2d46aca5c71f4b33fff1bf.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1.4)$ | $1.4$ | $(1.4,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈1.75.$ So ${f}^{″}(1.75)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(1.75)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈1.75,$ the slope is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b86a0a6f67463532a765c3ce85cb58da229ffa4e83ffd755610371e6c089b2b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa025d7b-74d5-5627-a7cd-12e3681b8b82" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96b43c66-9b1a-5b01-bcd2-cc8d2a256cbd" :answer/type {: