fied (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2.5)$ | $2.5$ | $(2.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fde284c6bda2a0a9dba79a41348cf25ce7d5e5db8d2af996c577ceca721d5ab2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97710476-c327-5b2d-9675-10f36b967d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Piecewise-Differentiable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2650ef17-0fcb-51fd-a9eb-eff25cdbe18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1e/1e83096f6dacd597fcab59f6dd7ae52f9a52133c59a5c6fb3f1bf0250b42032d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505171} {:answer/id #uuid "52c1b36d-6dec-5472-8e55-0c2af00b581a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1f92f42246d98eaa49b0acd36607de58a64bc4aff5e79487a175ecbf5d4ddcd8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505269} {:answer/id #uuid "8f56a479-bb17-5e09-be93-fcf9d34e974c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/da/da0643022b84affea31bd3f8dd8e7b3905b4fb908df46b4160ad24cce2499291.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505381} {:answer/id #uuid "b7564118-cc79-52d5-895d-26764e2194cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/71/7118cc00a570a15dd1cc4912f50705c7b3f27b231db041062b0670f7cd7bcb09.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505461} {:answer/id #uuid "ea91dcd2-b8e4-540e-a727-c7877e69d4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/9676ddaa53d496b0232940b54cbb618a901c2cdc730be0b203e3acc255732b77.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea91dcd2-b8e4-540e-a727-c7877e69d4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/9676ddaa53d496b0232940b54cbb618a901c2cdc730be0b203e3acc255732b77.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505561} :answer-field/id #uuid "c31977d0-78ff-535d-bc38-407ef7d45814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1651c23-1a6f-5db8-8bb9-875d0a212ede" :question/math-academy-id "q-51265" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6fe60368dc9dddb25380d76a34e8d3d7d2b2db4aa61e1d3333dda8242c37ccb0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from positive to negative) when we pass through $x=3$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=3$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/96/9676ddaa53d496b0232940b54cbb618a901c2cdc730be0b203e3acc255732b77.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97710476-c327-5b2d-9675-10f36b967d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Piecewise-Differentiable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "438cd5cc-aedc-50cc-ac57-0cc4ccfc5949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/017d8876b1dda0c8c9d94e28b93c12f756cd030d044a638a15e73a80da64f5b0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505240} {:answer/id #uuid "544e3cde-3241-57d1-8784-3d33f7e52e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/2060cb11e8f0402d74ef1baa3a287c7e9cef9ca58e6efa98923538cb53128f27.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505271} {:answer/id #uuid "914c8377-0df2-5fd6-876a-b3998921af10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1ca0e089b095330fb1441325c2a71a56423df60c6cb18acade6d75f06a040ef1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505384} {:answer/id #uuid "99c4a7c9-b8b6-5d1a-8631-bb06f3c8f9a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/31/31edaa2afe89ceccd2fe434d428ce333b909b0a46c6eeb14d216d254c062e8ff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505404} {:answer/id #uuid "9aefdebf-0e8a-59bd-bf33-5a57915efa2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/29/29e46d9b0874bc7c16031ae122890a0b4091db3c92174701b8ca1e5dae7f2917.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "914c8377-0df2-5fd6-876a-b3998921af10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1ca0e089b095330fb1441325c2a71a56423df60c6cb18acade6d75f06a040ef1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505384} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8e039b-69a8-5499-971f-81bd40925631" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3e7c579-7100-5dba-9db2-7857d6271cd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-51419" :question/problem "![](images/47/472b1c50cef2f5ce7be11fc83eda45775e734125ba0572305ab828dc7ed278b4.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Of the following, which could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following: \n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{not extr.}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also:\n\n- For $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down. So, ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing on this interval.\n\n- For $x\\in (-1,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is a increasing straight line. So, $y={f}^{′}(x)$ will be a positive constant on this interval.\n\nAdditionally, we have a rapid change of the slope of the tangent line when we pass through $x=-1$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=-1,$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/1c/1ca0e089b095330fb1441325c2a71a56423df60c6cb18acade6d75f06a040ef1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa025d7b-74d5-5627-a7cd-12e3681b8b82" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34f9b611-c085-5088-be1b-5b823e61cd6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e24bebcb155c9426d8dd4cb86bd8addc9ca937b8bf04963212bdfa61d7ea633.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505200} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce63a6f-67f0-559c-a43b-38d10d5e8213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9f/9f9206d1c2aaa0bd07ad5009b71cd8d67dca142455d04c465b4ece62fde040a2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505294} {:answer/id #uuid "709ab897-b72c-5b93-939f-47412cd95859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ca/ca891346b62abcda7da1594ffc8002ea7ea8d04ac3eb7e50f9e55f8f8439aba8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505334} {:answer/id #uuid "7afb0362-9b62-5073-9761-339ddea22967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/41c53ff7dd6fe96016fe5eb680ef83c9ce3d39498b684c1d9cf22dd506ed9493.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505352} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f51bb4-73de-517b-842f-54fc9611a7b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/58c6913f3c332443253fc8007ac5889e14390e1dbb0e6d66c9f89946eb3421c8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ce63a6f-67f0-559c-a43b-38d10d5e8213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9f/9f9206d1c2aaa0bd07ad5009b71cd8d67dca142455d04c465b4ece62fde040a2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505294} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f59a53d-e0c2-5a2f-a650-6f9d2dd21cde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e93d51a5-9dcf-5b93-bb29-2d6994b5330e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79362" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c11e098426544491bf77324dc380997c5883eff6ea5b589d75bd80055ecc3d83.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1.5)$ | $-1.5$ | $(-1.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9f9206d1c2aaa0bd07ad5009b71cd8d67dca142455d04c465b4ece62fde040a2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2ef7a61-79b9-5464-ac2a-3afb7dddd1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d9f20fb-fdb0-549d-b771-5508dba3311e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a68eb6c7dc24329ad71732682f0b489e0315e058a3928db5cbbcb9182c46cab0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505128} {:answer/id #uuid "18908474-09f9-51ca-b5a0-e606b75d57cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1bf02a93f611bece0e692ee238fa3d98769f22e178ffd9c1dfec7b7ab5f57ea4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505148} {:answer/id #uuid "548b733a-19c3-5703-9791-06c6b0d56ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c0/c0324d643e6f82390153e4991a9cdfd3391284778e7d9718dcb2a6b3d6572f08.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505272} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4912d0-4210-5162-87be-483c9f8d8900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7d4d71df487ab7302fbd485258c1b9431f3129aaf01df10f7b9c3f274f32e6b9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505325} {:answer/id #uuid "b510f5a7-36df-5f30-a9d5-ef5382cd43a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5d/5d6382c1736d5a2840a1db8f54e3c0ba9b39adc83ce48c61f30eac2fb1d4607e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d9f20fb-fdb0-549d-b771-5508dba3311e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a68eb6c7dc24329ad71732682f0b489e0315e058a3928db5cbbcb9182c46cab0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505128} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b51f657-f4fd-5037-87ae-5da0475c5c99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2c0c91e8-a70d-5113-bcb2-8f7a22529a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-79414" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f03e226c0d65852c48ef5136131db98b563da6885cb9930f6227e7908aba584.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the critical points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the critical points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has an inflection point at $x≈0.$ At the inflection point, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈0.$\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${g}^{′}(0)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangent to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈0$ is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a68eb6c7dc24329ad71732682f0b489e0315e058a3928db5cbbcb9182c46cab0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2ef7a61-79b9-5464-ac2a-3afb7dddd1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c82e3fc-15f7-5054-be9d-ba1e88ee764a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d7/d7d32a47a04bca11ba9321256de9c23ce24a7e79bd4495c12028b658602d88de.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505216} {:answer/id #uuid "8761dacd-e9a3-5e7e-a6c3-02a68914b77e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ce/ce7e8b147cc2fa4eb7959787d10b0ea635e5ac6411cdb0df9626a410d7d0589f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505369} {:answer/id #uuid "979f0cc0-6520-56fc-81a4-dda171bee116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c1/c16ffbba0310799c36ee1f21359b96f93f73ad121b7d5770784fbf6033ca0446.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505399} {:answer/id #uuid "d442c6ea-c924-5f33-ba89-5f1580048fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/24/246f2bf42fb02412bb06fbec262e2c30abcb0286f5419ff51b207e43acff6736.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505515} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e63383-4a56-5400-a091-38b716d1f0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2d/2dcf5fe5acbaa9ed150dd31e76390f50288165b68b1c7d9f77a340222bb0f6a7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "979f0cc0-6520-56fc-81a4-dda171bee116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c1/c16ffbba0310799c36ee1f21359b96f93f73ad121b7d5770784fbf6033ca0446.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505399} :answer-field/id #uuid "945133ec-d03d-5f9a-a393-2a2fb159bf47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0028aa3d-01ab-5dd0-b507-a0e026d36abf" :question/math-academy-id "q-79422" :question/problem "![](images/74/74a33fb06f8ef0f2f9f7935f5c53209392f3289e20be803b9d82737a46b31c06.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=g(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=g(x)$ is concave down in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ and $y={g}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c16ffbba0310799c36ee1f21359b96f93f73ad121b7d5770784fbf6033ca0446.png)"}]}
